---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const modalCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Necessity vs possibility', note: 'Necessary: true in all possible worlds (2+2=4). Possible: true in at least one (you could have been a chef). Contingent: true here, false elsewhere (the Eiffel Tower\'s location).' },
    { math: '2 · Possible worlds as a tool', note: 'Kripke semantics (1959, at age 19): model necessity/possibility with "accessibility" between worlds. Rigorous mathematics replaced vagueness — modal logic became a working instrument.' },
    { math: '3 · What it unlocked', note: 'Deontic logic (obligation/permission in law), temporal logic (always/eventually — used in program verification), epistemic logic (knowledge/belief), and counterfactual reasoning (Stalnaker/Lewis semantics for "if...had been").' },
    { math: '4 · Real applications in computing', note: 'Modal logic verifies hardware and protocols: "from every reachable state, the system never deadlocks" is a modal claim, checked exhaustively (model checking — Clarke, Emerson, Sifakis, Nobel-adjacent Turing Award 2007).' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/advanced/modal-logic">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Modal logic formalises <strong>necessity and possibility — the grammar of "must", "can" and
    "could have been"</strong>. After Kripke's semantics (1959), it became a precision instrument
    used far beyond philosophy: verifying computer chips, modelling legal obligations, encoding
    knowledge in AI systems, and making metaphysical arguments exact.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the machinery and where it actually runs" widget="equationStep" config={modalCfg} />

  <h2>Systems of modal logic</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Different axioms, different logics:</strong> K (base), T (what is necessary is true), S4 (necessity is necessary), S5 (necessity is known globally). Each fits a different modality — obligation, knowledge, time, proof.</li>
    <li><strong>The quantified extension matters most:</strong> quantified modal logic underlies de re/de dicto distinctions ("necessarily, the morning star is the evening star" — ambiguous in ways that shaped metaphysics).</li>
    <li><strong>Proof theory and decidability:</strong> which modal systems can be automated? Most useful ones are decidable — the reason model checking can exhaustively verify a chip design (a task with more states than atoms in the universe, made tractable by the mathematics).</li>
    <li><strong>The philosophical payoff:</strong> modal realism (Lewis's other worlds) vs actualism is the big metaphysical split; both sides now argue with Kripke's formalism rather than hand-waving.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kripke, "A Completeness Theorem in Modal Logic" (1959); Chellas, Modal Logic; Clarke, Emerson & Sifakis Turing Award (2007).</p>
</TopicPage>
